   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to eligible investments in foreign equity securities by public retirement systems of the state",
    "basePrintNo": "S4005",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4005-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §177, R & SS L",
    "lawSection": "Retirement and Social Security Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4005\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the retirement and social security law, in relation to\neligible investments in foreign equity securities by public retirement\nsystems of the state\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo permit public retirement systems of New York State to invest in the\naggregate up to twelve and a half percent of assets of the fund in\nforeign equity securities.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends subdivision 8 of Section 117 of the Retirement and\nSocial Security Law to increase the legal allowable investment from\nten percent to twelve and a half percent of assets in foreign equity\nsecurities.\n\nEXISTING LAW:\n\nSubdivision 8 of Section of the Retirement and Social Security Law is\nthe provision in the legal list which expressly authorizes investments\nin foreign equity securities. Investments under this subdivision are\ncurrently limited to ten percent of a system's assets and the\nstatutory provisions have not been amended since 1993.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPublic employee retirement systems may only make investments to the\nextent permitted by law. Today, retirement systems rely principally\nupon the investment authorization contained in Section 177 of the\nRetirement and Social Security Law, the so-called \"legal list.\" The\nauthorizations in the statute have been expanded over time to reflect\nchanges in the universe of investable capital assets and trends in\ninstitutional investment. The last change in the legal list occurred\nin 2010 with the enactment of Chapter 554 of the Laws of 2010 which\nincreased from five to ten percent the amount of assets that a public\nretirement system of the state may invest in real estate. This change\nhas proven to be beneficial to the public retirement systems.\n\nAs on June 30, 2014, the System's international equity portfolio\nreturned 22 percent with a five-year return of 11 percent The\nallocation of assets within the System's portfolio, as well as the\nfund's overall structure, are continuously reviewed and periodically\nadjust to achieve prudent diversification of assets across a broad\nspectrum of capital market segments. As the international markets\nconsistently perform within expectations, institutional investors will\nincreasingly seek to capture global equity opportunities.\nInstitutional investors typically will look to a broad and investable\nglobal equity benchmark, such as the MSCI ACWI IMI (Investable Market\nIndex) or Blended MSCI ACWI (ex-US) index), to not only benchmark\nequity portfolio performance, but to achieve broad based global\ncapitalization in the foreign public equities space. Increasing the\nlegal authorization in this asset class will more closely align with\n\n\nthe System's current target allocation to international equities which\nis at 18 percent with a target range of 14-20 percent.\n\nAdditionally, the proposed amendment to subdivision 8 of Section 177\nof the Retirement and Social Security Law represents the first such\namendment to this statutory provision in 20 years.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no estimated fiscal implications.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
